ref 17592186119668 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119853 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "c2c1a562-1b3d-5180-9fef-e20ea916b17a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120001 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120653 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120001 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120654 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120001 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120001 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120001 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120758 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120001 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120001 #edb/ref 1045 "LU Factorization of 2x2 Matrices" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120001 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186120653 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120001 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048743 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120001 #edb/ref 1050 720.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120001 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "288fdc58-d951-55d7-87ce-92acf93dd2ce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120653 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120653 #edb/ref 1061 17355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120653 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cca0ee93-f5ae-5db9-98de-167dafd23a32" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120653 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121028 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120654 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120654 #edb/ref 1061 17356 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120654 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9ff11c4f-9a18-5d14-b5c7-e64d00a7d65b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120654 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119792 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120655 #edb/ref 1061 17359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120655 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bb1d2bfc-7ab9-5e32-ba3e-2e7219f0bcc9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120655 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119793 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120746 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120758 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120746 #edb/ref 1061 23125 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120746 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ccd98e02-0c2a-5392-995e-e13e8079f540" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120746 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119853 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120758 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120654 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120758 #edb/ref 1061 23628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120758 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e8257db3-4571-507c-aee8-af086a0be8da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120758 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121067 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121028 #edb/ref 1064 "In the same way that we can factor numbers or polynomials, we can factor a matrix by writing it as a product of two or more matrices. In fact, there is one factorization technique that works for *all* matrices, called **LU factorization** (or **LU decomposition**).\n\nLU factorization involves writing a matrix $A$ as a product of two matrices $L$ and $U,$ where\n\n- ![](images/ad/adca162da15cab5989e5726acb495fc12533231c2e38b0392d68388aeb18493b.svg) is a *unit lower triangular matrix* (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal), and\n\n- ![](images/93/93338df4d1ee1791dc5b1679dbddff34d94d05677c362b1a41dcb925ca83a1dc.svg) is an *upper triangular matrix*.\n\nFor example, we can write the matrix ![](images/83/835b8ad372998cbb35be4c1998d236fe96f1d1b03ee1ad7062bdbbb77e254bf6.svg) in LU factored form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nYou may be wondering how we arrived at this factorization. Shortly, we'll discuss an algorithm that explains how to compute $L$ and $U$ for any $2\\times2$ matrix $A.$ But for now, let's focus on recognizing LU factored forms." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121028 #edb/ref 1065 3176 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121028 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "827a0fea-203f-5c8c-bb6a-4d0764f6de04" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121028 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121067 #edb/ref 1064 "Let ![](images/ff/ff7b3f4bca1676cbe3fb78ae1c2a47d8fd0bc71dd98dee4c7b6fea4cc94ec7bd.svg) To perform LU factorization, we need to write $A$ as \n\n![](images/3f/3f74fdde04340f6a4dbd86962f5a31f8af22b5fb8053c66fa3e8be41629b12a1.svg)\n\nTo find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Caution:** To get a unit lower triangular matrix at the end, we have the following restriction while reducing a matrix $A$ to row echelon form:\n\n- we can only add a multiple of the $1$st row to the $2$nd row ($R_2:=R_2+k R_1$), and\n\n- the other elementary row operations (swapping or multiplying a row by a number) are not allowed." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121067 #edb/ref 1065 3977 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121067 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4f9a2842-c4f1-577c-827c-41e33f514c31" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121067 #edb/ref 1067 "An Algorithm for Finding the LU Factorization of a 2x2 Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186